course: world
lesson: 8
title: Eclipses
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: An eclipse happens when the Sun, the Earth and the Moon stand in one straight line, so that one of them casts its shadow on another.
explain: In a solar eclipse the Moon passes between the Sun and the Earth and its shadow falls on the Earth: for a few minutes the Sun is hidden and day turns almost into night, but only along a narrow strip of the Earth.
explain: In a lunar eclipse the Earth passes between the Sun and the Moon and the Earth's shadow falls on the Moon: the Moon goes dim and often turns coppery red, and everyone on the night side of the Earth can see it.
explain: The Moon is about 400 times smaller across than the Sun but also about 400 times closer, so in our sky they look almost the same size, which is why the Moon can just cover the Sun, like a coin held near your eye covering a far-away house.
explain: There is no eclipse every month because the Moon's path is tilted a little, so usually it passes just above or below the line between the Sun and the Earth.
fact: An eclipse happens when the Sun, the Earth and the Moon are in a straight line.
fact: In a solar eclipse the Moon is between the Sun and the Earth.
fact: In a lunar eclipse the Earth is between the Sun and the Moon.
fact: A solar eclipse can happen only at new moon.
fact: A lunar eclipse can happen only at full moon.
fact: A total solar eclipse is seen only along a narrow strip of the Earth and lasts only a few minutes in each place.
fact: A lunar eclipse can be seen from the whole night side of the Earth and can last more than an hour.
fact: During a total lunar eclipse the Moon often looks red, because a little sunlight is bent through the Earth's air onto it.
fact: The Sun is about 400 times wider than the Moon and about 400 times farther away, so they look almost the same size in the sky.
fact: Eclipses do not happen every month because the Moon's path around the Earth is slightly tilted.
fact: You must never look at a solar eclipse without special eclipse glasses.
fact: Looking at a lunar eclipse is safe, because it is only the Moon.
exercise: What happens in a solar eclipse? | The Moon passes between the Sun and the Earth | The Earth passes between the Sun and the Moon | The Sun passes between the Earth and the Moon | A cloud passes in front of the Sun
exercise: What happens in a lunar eclipse? | The Earth's shadow falls on the Moon | The Moon's shadow falls on the Earth | The Sun's shadow falls on the Moon | The Moon stops reflecting light
exercise: At which phase of the Moon can a solar eclipse happen? | New moon | Full moon | Half moon | Any phase at all
exercise: At which phase of the Moon can a lunar eclipse happen? | Full moon | New moon | Crescent moon | Any phase at all
exercise: Why can the small Moon cover the huge Sun? | The Sun is about 400 times farther away | The Moon is bigger than the Sun | The Sun shrinks during an eclipse | The Moon's shadow is bigger than the Sun
exercise: Why does the Moon often look red in a lunar eclipse? | A little sunlight is bent through the Earth's air onto it | The Moon heats up and glows red | The Moon is covered with red dust that shows up only then | Mars shines its red light on it
exercise: Why is there not an eclipse every month? | The Moon's path is tilted, so it usually misses the line | The Moon only goes all the way around the Earth once a year | The Sun hides from the Moon most months | Eclipses need clear weather on the Moon
exercise: Which eclipse can more people see at once? | A lunar eclipse, seen from all the night side | A solar eclipse, seen from all the day side | Both are seen by exactly the same people | Neither can be seen without a telescope
exercise: How long does a total solar eclipse last in one place? | Only a few minutes | A whole day and night | About one whole week | About a whole month
exercise: How must you watch a solar eclipse? | Only through special eclipse glasses | Through ordinary sunglasses | Straight with your eyes for a short time | Through binoculars without a filter
exercise: Is it safe to look at a lunar eclipse? | Yes, because it is only the dim Moon | No, it can damage your eyes | Only with eclipse glasses | Only through a telescope
exercise: Why does it get dark during a total solar eclipse? | The Moon's shadow falls on the place | The Sun stops shining for a while | The Earth's shadow falls on the Sun | Thick clouds always come at that time
exercise: What must be in a straight line for an eclipse? | The Sun, the Earth and the Moon | The Sun, Mars and the Earth | The Moon, the stars and the Earth | The Sun, the Moon and Venus
exercise: How is a coin near your eye like the Moon in an eclipse? | Small but close, it can hide something big and far | It is round, so it makes the sky round | It is shiny, so it reflects the light of the Sun into your eye | It is cold, so it cools the Sun
test: Kai holds a ball between a lamp and a globe. What is he showing? | A solar eclipse | A lunar eclipse | A full moon | The seasons
test: Tonight there is a half moon. Which is true? | No eclipse can happen tonight | A lunar eclipse could happen tonight | A solar eclipse could happen tonight | Both eclipses could happen tonight
test: If the Moon were twice as far away, how would solar eclipses change? | The Moon would look too small to cover the whole Sun | The Moon would cover the Sun for much longer | They would happen every month | They would be seen from the whole Earth
test: In one town the Sun goes dark for a few minutes, while a city 2 thousand kilometres away sees nothing special. What is happening? | A solar eclipse | A lunar eclipse | A new moon | A sunset
test: Astronauts on the Moon look at the Earth during a lunar eclipse. What do they see? | The Earth hiding the Sun, with a red ring of light around it | The Moon's dark round shadow sliding slowly across the Earth below | A full Earth shining brightly | Nothing unusual at all
test: A friend wants to watch a solar eclipse through dark sunglasses. What should you tell him? | They are not safe; only special eclipse glasses are | They are safe if he looks only quickly | They are safe if the glasses are very dark | They are safe because the Moon blocks the heat
test: Which is true about the shadow in a lunar eclipse? | It is round, because the Earth is a ball | It is square, because of the shapes of the continents | It is a thin straight line across the Moon | It is the shadow of the Sun falling on the Moon
test: Why does a lunar eclipse last much longer than a total solar eclipse in one place? | The Earth's big shadow takes long to cross the small Moon | The Moon moves faster during a lunar eclipse | The Sun goes behind the Earth for the night | A lunar eclipse happens in winter only
test: If the Moon's path were not tilted at all, what would happen? | There would be eclipses every month | There would be no eclipses ever | The Moon would have no phases | The Moon would stop going around the Earth
test: wot's a solar eclipse then | The Moon gets between the Sun and the Earth | The Earth gets between the Sun and the Moon | The Sun goes behind a big cloud | The Sun switches off for a few minutes
test: can i look at the eclipse tmrw with my sunnies | No, only special eclipse glasses are safe for a solar eclipse | Yes, dark sunglasses are enough | Yes, if you only look for a minute | Yes, the Moon blocks all the harmful light, so sunnies are fine
test: my sister says we get a solar eclipse every new moon. true? | No, the Moon's path is tilted so it usually misses the line | Yes, every single new moon makes one | Yes, but only when it's cloudy | No, solar eclipses happen at full moon
test: Tom reckons the Moon goes red in a lunar eclipse cos it gets hot. right? | No, a little sunlight bends through Earth's air onto it | Yes, the Moon heats up in the Earth's shadow | Yes, it glows red like hot metal | No, it's red because of rusty dust that covers it all over
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a solar eclipse? | The Moon is interposed between the Sun and the Earth | The Earth is interposed between the Sun and the Moon | The Sun is interposed between the Earth and the Moon | A cloud is interposed between the Sun and the Earth
exam: State the lunar phase at which, and at which alone, a lunar eclipse may occur. | Full moon | New moon | First quarter | Last quarter
exam: Identify the principal reason for which eclipses do not occur in every month. | The orbit of the Moon is slightly inclined | The Moon moves too rapidly to come into alignment | The Sun is too large to be obscured by the Moon | The Earth's atmosphere conceals them from view
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the safe observation of eclipses. | A solar eclipse requires special eclipse glasses; a lunar eclipse does not | A lunar eclipse requires special glasses; a solar eclipse does not | Both may be observed safely with the naked eye | Neither may be observed safely by any means
